{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787151249,"nonce":"t7uWxYLbV0lAciflVpPeSj8/A+Q=","message":"**A premise correction first, then the decision laid out so it can be made in five minutes rather than re-derived.**\n\n⚠️ **`generation` DOES have an advance trigger, so this ticket's headline is half wrong — and the half that is right is worse than it sounds.** `internal/grouping/repository/group.go:211-238` computes the next generation IN THE STATEMENT: `COALESCE((SELECT max(prev.generation) FROM alert_groups prev WHERE prev.org_id = $2 AND prev.group_key = $5), 0) + 1`, under `groups_key_gen_uniq`, with its own comment explaining that reading the max in Go would be the classic read-then-write race — *\"two live generations of one group — two Slack threads for one conversation\"*. It fires when the next fact for a `group_key` finds no open generation. I read this myself rather than taking it from a report.\n\nSo the mechanism exists. **What does not exist is a mechanism for the thing that REPLACES it.** `OpenGeneration` belongs to `alert_groups`, and the owner's one-Case-per-conversation ruling (`7570090`, comment `7e547a0`) supersedes `alert_groups` entirely. The rollover gap is in the replacement, not in today's code — which means this ticket cannot be worked before `7570090`'s shape is settled, and it also means nothing is currently broken in production. That reframing matters for sequencing.\n\n**The candidate axes, with what each already has in the tree:**\n\n- **(a) idle time — reuse `group_close_delay_s`.** The attraction is one number governing one concept, and no new tuning surface. But it is silent on both pathological cases: an alert that flaps every thirty seconds never goes idle, and an alert that fires once and never resolves has no idle signal either. It answers *\"should oto stop watching this alert-group\"*, which is an ingest question, not *\"has this conversation gone on long enough to be worth re-rooting\"*, which is a legibility question.\n- **(b) a reply-count ceiling.** oto already has this infrastructure, shipped and tested: `MaxThreadReplies = 30` and `NeedsFreshRoot`, with `reasonContinued` rendering *\"Continued in a new message — this thread reached 30 replies\"*. ⛔ But today it re-roots while PRESERVING conversation identity (`ClearPointer`, `threads.go:281-294`) — it deliberately does not end anything. Adopting it as the rollover axis changes what that mechanism MEANS, and that is the decision, not a detail.\n- **(c) wall-clock age.** No existing code, and the only axis that bounds both pathologies unconditionally.\n- **(d) (b) OR (c), whichever fires first.** One new column, no real precedence conflict.\n\n**The recommendation is (d)**, on the grounds that (a) fails exactly the two cases a rollover rule exists for, and that (b) alone leaves a thread that reaches thirty replies in a week indistinguishable from one that reaches thirty in an hour.\n\n⭐ **The one question only you can answer:** is the rollover threshold protecting **Slack thread legibility** (reply-count), a **maximum conversation lifetime** (wall-clock age), or both — and does the existing 30-reply re-root become the SAME event as rollover, or a separate smaller one nested inside it? Everything else follows mechanically from that.\n\n**Blocked behind this:** `7570090` stages 4-7 (stage 6 depends on it directly), and behind those, `23303a0` and `b1e26f7`. Four tickets on one answer.\n\nFull memo, with every citation and the pathological-case walkthroughs: `~/.afk/oto/20260819-1920/decision-7287b28-rollover.md`.\n\n⚠️ **Not decided and not built.** An unattended session must not choose a product axis, and three in a row have now declined to. This comment exists so the fourth does not have to re-derive the problem before you answer.","files":null}]}